 {:seon.task/name "a-tests-verdict-holds-the-schema-entries-it-read"
  :seon.task/plan "Recorded reads for in-process tests (owner, 2026-11-10: build it). What exists: a test's digest is static, its reach plus the input classes its reached forms name (seon.program/graph-index, env-class-readers). seon.db/entity-index chooses an entity schema at run time by the attributes an entity holds, so no static name says which entity schemas a test reads; since 19076dfbbe every test reaching it takes the whole :seon.env/schemas class: about 1,160 of 1,614 tests rerun on any entity-schema edit (599 before), and 196 of 757 landings from 2026-10-27 to 2026-11-10 touched the schema resources. The gate: (1) ours: render already records what a component read and keeps it per component (seon.render's :reads, d/reads-index, the readers index a transaction is matched against); verdicts are files keyed by digest (seon.fx/write-verdicts!), reused by seon.proofs/plan. (2) v1 selected by static reach only (seon.test, test_reaching_test), with no run-time record. (3) The field: Bazel's unused_inputs_list (an action names the inputs it did not read, and the next key drops them), ninja's depfiles (the compiler writes the headers it read; the next build keys on them), Salsa (each query records the queries it read and is revalidated by their revisions). (4) It adds one field to a verdict file, :reads {schema key content digest}, and a second reuse step; the static digest cannot hold it because the choice is made at run time. The recorder exists: seon.ratom's tracking view (defview: ILookup, IFn, Associative, Seqable over a value, each read notified by its key path, a missing key as a :shallow read, an iteration as a :keys read of its path), which is how a render knows what it read. Its readers (surveyed 2026-11-10) read the entity index by key, (es a), (:schema-identities ix) of a schema, required-keys of a touched schema, except seon.db/reaching (iterates :targets) and the write check's cascade (keys of :identity-schemas, only on a whole-entity retraction): those record a :keys read, which reads every entry, honestly. Plan: in a test child, the entity index each reader takes (seon.db/index, index-of) is that view over the value, the test run in a context that collects the reads, so each schema key resolved and each MISS (a key no schema holds) is recorded at the read, never at a cache fill (the second of two tests in one process reads through the first's cache). The verdict gains :reads. For an in-process test the digest drops :seon.env/schemas (the class of 19076dfbbe), and a verdict at that digest is reused only when every recorded key's current form digest equals the recorded one and no recorded miss now matches a schema; a verdict with no :reads (written before) reruns once. Process tests keep the class: their reads happen in other processes. Proved at n = 3 (2026-11-10, a scratch test, the binary's index swapped for a view and each test run in a context collecting reads): seon.problems-test/a-root-on-a-stale-binary-is-a-problem read 53 index paths, all by key (an attribute to its owning schema: a handful of entity schemas); seon.task-test/deleting-a-cited-var-is-refused-naming-its-cites read 54 by key plus the whole of :identity-schemas (the cascade on a whole-entity retraction, as surveyed); seon.problems-test/health-reads-each-part-once read none of the binary's index (it takes another: find which before building). The recorded path [:entity-schemas a] maps to the schema owning a, [:required-keys s] and [:forms s] to s, a :keys read of a top-level map to every entry. Estimates to measure before building past the recorder (stop if any is over 2x): tests moved by an edit of :seon.cite/cite-fact 150-250, of the agent's fact 300-500, of the eval's fact 250-400 (in-process only), against about 1,160 today. Cost line: the recorder is one set conj per lookup (n = lookups a test makes); the reuse check is one form-digest compare per recorded key, read from the schema entries once per plan. Proofs, red first: a test that reads only app.calc's forms is reused when an unrelated entity schema changes and reruns when a schema it matched changes; two tests in one process both record a schema the first one's lookup cached; a recorded miss reruns when a new schema matches it; a verdict without :reads reruns once. Wiki: docs/merging.md#verdicts-are-a-cache and docs/source.md#tests-run-by-reach."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
